    "stimulusText": "2\n\nThe following text applies to the questions at positions 13, 14, 15, 16, 17\n\nInstructions\n\nQuestions 13 -17 relate to Case 5\n\nCase 5\n\nVarious structures are visible on the chest radiograph because of the density difference between them and the anatomically adjacent aerated lung. The replacement of alveolar air by soft tissue density, such as secretions, inflammatory fluid or oedema, will remove the density gradient and the borders will no longer be distinguishable, known as the silhouette sign.\n\nIt is important to understand the lobar anatomy and thus the location of infection when interpreting the radiograph. The following five questions will help you practise this skill.",
